  <entry>
    <title>[Open Agricola Development Diary] EP 1: Why Rebuild Agricola?</title>
    <link href="http://titanxxh.com/en/2026/02/22/why-rebuild-agricola-origin-and-bga-code-smells/"/>
    <id>http://titanxxh.com/en/2026/02/22/why-rebuild-agricola-origin-and-bga-code-smells/</id>
    <published>2026-02-22T07:15:32.000Z</published>
    <updated>2026-08-29T15:08:47.050Z</updated>
    
    
    <summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;Agricola is more than a decade old. By today’s standards, it is not especially heavy. How hard could a complete digital version really be?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Its rulebook is not frighteningly thick. The trouble is that a lot of traps can fit inside one sentence. Take Hammer Crusher:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Immediately before you renovate to stone, you get 2 clay and 1 reed and you can take a “Build Rooms” action.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I glanced at that text and thought: what is so hard about this? Hand out the resources, let the player build, done. Once a program has to run it, the questions arrive immediately. Exactly when does it trigger? Can someone who cannot yet afford a stone renovation choose Renovation in the first place? How many more traps can you spot? I spotted none the first time. I will come back to them later.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This afternoon, I made the first commit to Open Agricola. Before I started, my thoughts kept returning to the card that had already caused me so much pain.&lt;/p&gt;</summary>
    
    
    
