  <entry>
    <title>Notes on Compression Algorithms</title>
    <link href="http://titanxxh.com/en/2020/12/26/notes-on-compression-algorithms/"/>
    <id>http://titanxxh.com/en/2020/12/26/notes-on-compression-algorithms/</id>
    <published>2020-12-26T15:32:43.000Z</published>
    <updated>2026-08-22T12:37:57.005Z</updated>
    
    
    <summary type="html">&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Know how to solve every problem that has been solved.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What I cannot create I do not understand.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;–Richard P. Feynman&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An internal competition at work forced me to learn several compression algorithms within a short time last month. I also implemented some of them, including lz77, deflate, bwt, and bcm. You never know until you practice: the moment you begin writing code, you discover that some things you thought you understood were never truly understood. I also recently watched a series of videos about the late physicist Richard Feynman. One detail left a deep impression. After his death, people found that he had preserved the two sentences above in the upper-left corner of his office blackboard, where they would not be erased accidentally.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;From a young age, Feynman’s father taught him that knowing and understanding were two different things. He therefore retained the curiosity to think through every problem.&lt;br&gt;The two sentences express essentially the same idea: only when you can create something yourself do you truly understand it. Reaching that point requires knowing how every problem was actually solved, not merely remembering a conclusion. The highest level of understanding a concept is being able to teach it to somebody else.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Inspired by that idea, I wrote this article to record my recent exploration of compression algorithms.&lt;/p&gt;</summary>
    
    
    
